+# Tally FAQ for newcomers
+
+## How do I install the command line tool?
+
+You need Python 3.11 or newer. Install the tool with pipx: `pipx install tally-toolkit`. Check it with `tallyctl version`. If you get "command not found", run `pipx ensurepath` and open a new terminal.
+
+## What is the tool called now?
+
+`tallyctl`. It was called `tally-cli` until the 2026-09 release. The old name still works as an alias for one more release and will then be removed.
+
+## Which port does tally listen on?
+
+Port 8731. The old port 8080 is closed since the 2026-09 release, so anything still pointing at it gets a refused connection.
+
+## How do I clear the cache?
+
+Run `tallyctl cache clear`. It clears all regions by default; add `--region NAME` to clear only one. The old purge command has been removed.
+
+## How long until an unanswered page escalates?
+
+15 minutes without acknowledgement. The page goes first to the primary on-call, then to the secondary, then to the engineering manager.
+
+## Does the pager wake me at night?
+
+Only for severity 1, meaning customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Between 22:00 and 07:00, severity 2 incidents don't page. They wait until morning, but keep watching the dashboards.
+
+## What do I do when the ingest queue is stuck?
+
+Run `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` first and look for the cause afterwards. A plain restart picks up the same poisoned batch again. The drain parks that batch in the dead-letter table so you can inspect it later.
+
+## What do I do when the API certificate has expired?
+
+Renew it with `tallyctl certs renew api`, then restart the service with `tallyctl restart api`. The new certificate is only served after the restart. `tallyctl certs list` shows expiry dates.
